course: dictionary
lesson: 12274
title: Spotting contradictions
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A contradiction happens when two statements or facts cannot both be true. If one friend says the match was on Monday and another says it was on Friday, they cannot both be right. Spotting a contradiction helps you check whether a story makes sense.
explain: Look for clues that two things clash. «Sam said the shop was open, but later he said it was closed.» The two statements contradict each other, so one of them must be false.
explain: Contradict can also be a verb. If Mia says, «The bell rang at noon», and Tom answers, «No, it did not», Tom is contradicting her: he is saying the opposite of what she said.
fact: A contradiction is when two statements or facts cannot both be true.
fact: «A quiet shout» is a contradiction in terms, because quiet and shout seem to oppose each other.
fact: In maths and logic, a statement that can never be true is a contradiction.
fact: Detectives look for contradictions in a suspect's story to find out who is lying.
fact: «Proof by contradiction» shows that something is true by showing that its opposite leads to nonsense.
exercise: Which pair of statements is a contradiction? | The door is made of wood | *The door is open and the door is closed | The door is red | The door is heavy
exercise: «Ella said she was at home all day, but her photo was taken at the beach.» What is the contradiction? | She took a photo | The beach is near her home | *Her two statements cannot both be true | Both places are nice
exercise: Which of these is a contradiction in terms? | A cold winter | *A deafening silence | A bright day | A happy tune
test: Which word means saying the opposite of what someone else has said? | Agree | Whisper | *Contradict | Repeat
test: Which of these can never be true? | Triangles have three sides | Cats like milk | *Two plus two is both four and not four | The sun rises in the east
exam: What does «a contradiction in terms» mean? | A long story told in order | A phrase with lots of rhymes | *A phrase whose parts seem to oppose each other | A sentence that is written in capital letters
